messages:
Priya: Hey team — customers started hitting 401 invalid_api_key errors after the frontend deploy an hour ago. Only happens on pages that have the service worker enabled.

Priya: Example client call (React app):
```js
fetch('/api/predict', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Authorization': `ApiKey ${window.__RD_KEY__}`,
    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ prompt })
})
```

Priya: On the server access logs we see requests from those clients with no Authorization header at all — returns 401. Other clients (no SW) are fine.

support-bot: Automatic: 401 invalid_api_key detected for route /v1/predict (count: 42 in last 30m). See ticket #S-5492.

Sam: Thanks Priya. Can you paste the exact service worker fetch handler? Sounds like SW might be altering the request.

Priya: Sure — here's the relevant snippet from our deployed sw.js (minified slightly):
```js
self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
  if (event.request.url.includes('/api/')) {
    event.respondWith(
      fetch(new Request(event.request, { headers: { 'x-analytics': 'true' } }))
    );
  }
});
```

Priya: We added that x-analytics header in the SW to tag traffic. Didn't realize it would affect auth. :facepalm:

Ingrid: That is the issue — new Request(event.request, { headers: { ... } }) creates a Request with the provided headers only, it does NOT merge existing headers. So Authorization gets dropped.

Ingrid: Quick repro locally: replace with the above code and you'll see the server's request.headers.Authorization === undefined.

Sam: Good catch. What's the safe fix?

Ingrid: Two options: 1) merge headers from event.request into a Headers instance and set the new header, or 2) use event.request.clone() and construct a Request with merged headers. Example: 
```js
self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
  if (event.request.url.includes('/api/')) {
    const headers = new Headers(event.request.headers);
    headers.set('x-analytics', 'true');
    const req = new Request(event.request, { headers });
    event.respondWith(fetch(req));
  }
});
```

Kari: Also a reminder — never rely on client-side Authorization for long-lived keys. In our architecture we recommend proxying auth through your backend or using ephemeral tokens. But for this bug the header-preservation fix is fine.

Priya: Implemented Ingrid's header-merge fix, pushed a hotfix (PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/app/pull/542) and rolled to prod.

Priya: After the fix the 401 spike subsided. Confirming in logs: Authorization present and calls succeed. Thanks!

Sam: Awesome. Can you paste the before/after access-log snippets for the runbook?

Priya: Before:
```
-> GET /v1/predict 401 - headers: { host: 'api.redwood.ai', user-agent: 'Mozilla/...' }
```
After:
```
-> GET /v1/predict 200 - headers: { host: 'api.redwood.ai', authorization: 'ApiKey ABC...xyz', user-agent: 'Mozilla/...' }
```

Sam: Adding note to the support runbook: "If 401s only on SW-enabled clients, inspect service worker fetch handlers for header replacement (new Request with explicit headers overwrites Authorization)."

support-bot: Ticket #S-5492 updated: root cause identified (service worker header overwrite). Status: resolved.

Priya: Thanks everyone — rolling this pattern to other repos now.
